  summary: "The ordinal utility approach holds that utility cannot be measured absolutely, but consumption bundles can be ranked according to preference as 1st, 2nd and 3rd. Its assumptions are rationality, ordinal utility, a diminishing marginal rate of substitution, total utility depending on quantities consumed, and consistency and transitivity of choice. Consistency prevents reversing a ranking under the same conditions, and transitivity guarantees that if A is preferred to B and B to C, then A is preferred to C.",
